WEBVTT

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:03.210
The first night in your own apartment feels different.

00:00:03.790 --> 00:00:05.370
You put your keys on the counter.

00:00:05.870 --> 00:00:07.870
You leave your shoes wherever you want.

00:00:08.370 --> 00:00:10.150
Nobody asks when you'll be home.

00:00:10.730 --> 00:00:13.270
For a moment, the silence feels like success.

00:00:14.230 --> 00:00:15.270
Then the bills arrive.

00:00:16.110 --> 00:00:18.305
Rent. Electricity. Internet.

00:00:18.330 --> 00:00:21.310
Groceries. And you notice something strange.

00:00:22.110 --> 00:00:24.370
There's only one person living here.

00:00:24.470 --> 00:00:27.310
But most of the bills don't look like half of anything.

00:00:28.130 --> 00:00:29.910
You use one bedroom.

00:00:30.570 --> 00:00:31.870
You watch one television.

00:00:32.550 --> 00:00:33.690
You eat one dinner.

00:00:34.130 --> 00:00:37.385
So why does living alone feel like you're paying for an entire

00:00:37.410 --> 00:00:42.110
household? Because, in several important ways, you are.

00:00:42.510 --> 00:00:46.605
To understand why it has become so expensive, we need to separate

00:00:46.630 --> 00:00:51.445
two things: prices going up, and the cost of having nobody to

00:00:51.470 --> 00:00:52.450
split them with.

00:00:52.890 --> 00:00:55.810
Imagine two apartments in the same building.

00:00:56.470 --> 00:00:59.870
Alex lives alone in a one-bedroom apartment.

00:01:00.250 --> 00:01:03.510
Across the hall, two friends share a two-bedroom.

00:01:03.790 --> 00:01:07.150
Let's use an illustrative budget, not a national average.

00:01:07.910 --> 00:01:11.130
Alex pays eighteen hundred dollars a month in rent.

00:01:11.810 --> 00:01:14.460
The two-bedroom costs twenty-four hundred.

00:01:14.870 --> 00:01:17.830
Split equally, that's twelve hundred dollars each.

00:01:18.610 --> 00:01:20.890
The shared apartment costs more overall.

00:01:21.330 --> 00:01:25.430
But each person pays six hundred dollars less than Alex.

00:01:26.490 --> 00:01:27.870
Now add internet.

00:01:28.350 --> 00:01:31.870
Suppose both apartments have the same sixty-dollar plan.

00:01:32.550 --> 00:01:34.210
Alex pays sixty.

00:01:34.750 --> 00:01:36.420
Each friend pays thirty.

00:01:36.870 --> 00:01:40.865
That puts Alex at eighteen hundred and sixty dollars for rent and

00:01:40.890 --> 00:01:44.972
internet. Each friend pays twelve hundred and thirty.

00:01:45.030 --> 00:01:48.332
A difference of six hundred and thirty dollars a month.

00:01:48.358 --> 00:01:52.790
Seven thousand five hundred and sixty dollars over a year.

00:01:53.890 --> 00:01:57.250
That's before comparing utilities or anything else.

00:01:57.850 --> 00:02:00.070
These aren't identical living arrangements.

00:02:00.790 --> 00:02:05.070
Alex gets privacy, and the friends share a kitchen and living room.

00:02:05.510 --> 00:02:09.510
Local prices could make the gap much smaller or much larger.

00:02:09.990 --> 00:02:15.045
But the mechanism is simple: sharing a household lets people divide some

00:02:15.070 --> 00:02:18.670
costs that don't double when a second person moves in.

00:02:19.250 --> 00:02:21.730
That sharing advantage isn't new.

00:02:22.810 --> 00:02:25.850
What's changed is the price of the things being shared.

00:02:26.650 --> 00:02:31.625
Take rent. The Bureau of Labor Statistics reported that its rent of

00:02:31.650 --> 00:02:36.845
primary residence index rose four point three percent over the twelve months

00:02:36.870 --> 00:02:39.273
ending December twenty twenty-four.

00:02:39.630 --> 00:02:43.905
That's a national measure of rent changes, not a quote for your

00:02:43.930 --> 00:02:49.410
next apartment. Your city, building, and lease can tell a different story.

00:02:50.510 --> 00:02:54.065
But it shows how a cost can keep getting bigger even after

00:02:54.090 --> 00:02:56.370
the fastest inflation has passed.

00:02:57.050 --> 00:03:00.630
Slower price growth still means prices are rising.

00:03:01.130 --> 00:03:03.530
It doesn't mean the old price comes back.

00:03:04.110 --> 00:03:08.845
And when your rent increases, there's nobody across the breakfast table to

00:03:08.870 --> 00:03:10.125
share the increase with.

00:03:10.150 --> 00:03:13.830
Living alone is also far from unusual.

00:03:15.250 --> 00:03:20.170
In twenty twenty-four, the Census Bureau counted about thirty-eight point five million

00:03:20.240 --> 00:03:25.050
one-person households, roughly twenty-nine percent of all U.S.

00:03:25.310 --> 00:03:29.490
households. That's households, not twenty-nine percent of Americans.

00:03:30.550 --> 00:03:35.165
People live alone after a move, a breakup, a loss, or simply

00:03:35.190 --> 00:03:36.585
because they prefer it.

00:03:36.610 --> 00:03:43.265
Different stories. The same practical question: how much household can one income

00:03:43.290 --> 00:03:47.250
carry? Some costs shrink when you live alone.

00:03:48.130 --> 00:03:49.110
Others barely move.

00:03:49.710 --> 00:03:52.370
You probably use less water than two people.

00:03:52.590 --> 00:03:53.710
You buy less food.

00:03:54.390 --> 00:03:56.430
Your electricity use may be lower.

00:03:57.110 --> 00:03:59.770
But a refrigerator still runs all day.

00:04:00.310 --> 00:04:02.610
An internet connection still needs a plan.

00:04:03.310 --> 00:04:07.065
A couch doesn't come at half price because only one person sits

00:04:07.090 --> 00:04:10.680
on it. Even groceries involve tradeoffs.

00:04:11.750 --> 00:04:15.385
A larger package might cost less per serving, but only if you

00:04:15.410 --> 00:04:16.650
actually use it.

00:04:17.130 --> 00:04:20.345
The cheaper bag of spinach isn't cheaper if half of it ends

00:04:20.370 --> 00:04:21.290
up in the trash.

00:04:21.990 --> 00:04:25.150
Buying smaller portions can mean paying more per ounce.

00:04:25.770 --> 00:04:29.510
Buying bigger portions can mean paying for food you never eat.

00:04:30.110 --> 00:04:32.205
Then there are the costs of setting up a home.

00:04:32.230 --> 00:04:35.390
A mattress. Pots and pans.

00:04:35.990 --> 00:04:38.350
Cleaning supplies. A table.

00:04:39.590 --> 00:04:43.390
Some are one-time purchases, not monthly bills.

00:04:44.050 --> 00:04:47.945
But if they all arrive during your first month, your checking account

00:04:47.970 --> 00:04:49.830
feels the difference immediately.

00:04:50.590 --> 00:04:55.425
And a security deposit, even if it's refundable, ties up cash you

00:04:55.450 --> 00:04:56.510
can't use elsewhere.

00:04:57.610 --> 00:05:01.405
The rent advertised online is only one part of what it takes

00:05:01.430 --> 00:05:03.450
to move in and stay there.

00:05:03.890 --> 00:05:06.690
There's another cost that doesn't appear on the lease.

00:05:07.910 --> 00:05:09.310
The risk is concentrated.

00:05:10.190 --> 00:05:14.470
If Alex loses a job, the apartment still needs the full rent.

00:05:15.190 --> 00:05:17.650
The utility company still sends the bill.

00:05:18.230 --> 00:05:22.470
A household with two earners may have another paycheck to lean on.

00:05:22.990 --> 00:05:24.870
That isn't guaranteed protection.

00:05:25.630 --> 00:05:29.930
Both people can lose work, and two people also have more expenses.

00:05:30.690 --> 00:05:35.505
But living alone on one paycheck means there is no second household

00:05:35.530 --> 00:05:38.710
income automatically waiting in the background.

00:05:39.470 --> 00:05:41.350
Go back to Alex's example.

00:05:42.010 --> 00:05:47.750
Rent and internet alone need eighteen hundred and sixty dollars each month.

00:05:48.450 --> 00:05:52.598
Two months of just those bills would be thirty-seven hundred and twenty

00:05:52.623 --> 00:05:56.390
dollars. That's not a complete emergency fund target.

00:05:57.210 --> 00:06:02.910
It leaves out food, utilities, transportation, healthcare, and other obligations.

00:06:03.830 --> 00:06:05.510
It's a way to see the exposure.

00:06:06.190 --> 00:06:11.345
When one person carries the full household bill, protecting that income becomes

00:06:11.370 --> 00:06:13.250
part of the cost of independence.

00:06:14.190 --> 00:06:17.950
At this point, the obvious advice is: get a roommate.

00:06:18.810 --> 00:06:21.425
Sometimes, that is the right financial move.

00:06:21.450 --> 00:06:25.750
But people don't choose a home using a calculator alone.

00:06:26.790 --> 00:06:30.890
Living alone can mean sleeping properly after a night shift.

00:06:31.190 --> 00:06:33.770
Recovering from a difficult relationship.

00:06:34.310 --> 00:06:36.290
Having a quiet place to work.

00:06:36.750 --> 00:06:41.765
Feeling safe. Those benefits are real, even when you can't put a

00:06:41.790 --> 00:06:43.910
precise dollar amount on them.

00:06:44.470 --> 00:06:48.845
The useful question is whether the extra cost fits alongside the rest

00:06:48.870 --> 00:06:49.690
of your life.

00:06:50.410 --> 00:06:54.905
In Alex's example, the rent-and-internet difference is six hundred and thirty dollars

00:06:54.930 --> 00:06:59.550
a month. For someone else, it might be much less.

00:07:00.230 --> 00:07:01.070
Or much more.

00:07:01.970 --> 00:07:05.565
Once you estimate your own difference, you can decide what you're getting

00:07:05.590 --> 00:07:09.785
for it, what you're giving up, and whether that trade still feels

00:07:09.810 --> 00:07:13.710
worth making. Start with the full cost of your home.

00:07:14.350 --> 00:07:21.810
Before signing a lease, add rent, required fees, utilities, internet, renters insurance,

00:07:22.490 --> 00:07:25.430
and the transportation costs tied to that location.

00:07:26.370 --> 00:07:30.225
A cheaper apartment that requires a car can leave you spending more

00:07:30.250 --> 00:07:33.650
overall. List moving costs separately.

00:07:34.870 --> 00:07:39.505
Include deposits, basic furniture, and the money needed before the first normal

00:07:39.530 --> 00:07:40.970
month even begins.

00:07:41.830 --> 00:07:46.965
Then build a bare-bones monthly budget: the expenses you'd still need to

00:07:46.990 --> 00:07:48.790
cover if your income stopped.

00:07:49.430 --> 00:07:52.925
Use that number to start planning a cash buffer that fits your

00:07:52.950 --> 00:07:58.785
situation. Even a small reserve can help with a surprise bill; building

00:07:58.810 --> 00:08:00.150
one takes time.

00:08:00.850 --> 00:08:06.725
Next, examine the biggest recurring commitments before spending all your energy on

00:08:06.750 --> 00:08:13.445
tiny purchases. A smaller apartment, a different neighborhood, or a lower-cost internet

00:08:13.470 --> 00:08:15.130
plan may create room.

00:08:15.830 --> 00:08:20.830
Check the actual tradeoffs, including safety, commute, and moving expenses.

00:08:21.650 --> 00:08:24.970
For food, plan around ingredients you can finish.

00:08:26.010 --> 00:08:30.285
Freeze portions. Compare the cost of what you eat, not just the

00:08:30.310 --> 00:08:31.550
price on the package.

00:08:31.870 --> 00:08:34.130
And build a practical support network.

00:08:34.950 --> 00:08:38.970
Someone you can call when you're sick or need help getting home.

00:08:39.550 --> 00:08:44.070
Living independently doesn't mean handling every problem without anyone else.

00:08:44.730 --> 00:08:48.145
The next time Alex puts the keys on the counter, the apartment

00:08:48.170 --> 00:08:49.370
is still quiet.

00:08:50.050 --> 00:08:51.730
The privacy still matters.

00:08:52.370 --> 00:08:55.090
But now the budget includes more than the rent.

00:08:55.690 --> 00:09:00.245
It includes the smaller bills, the move-in costs, and a plan for

00:09:00.270 --> 00:09:02.010
the months that don't go smoothly.

00:09:02.950 --> 00:09:08.285
Living alone has become more expensive because higher prices meet a household

00:09:08.310 --> 00:09:11.090
that has fewer ways to share them.

00:09:11.610 --> 00:09:14.070
Understanding that won't make a lease cheaper.

00:09:14.670 --> 00:09:19.205
It can help explain why being careful with money sometimes still feels

00:09:19.230 --> 00:09:20.610
like falling behind.

00:09:21.450 --> 00:09:25.585
If living alone matters to you, give it an honest place in

00:09:25.610 --> 00:09:30.490
your budget. And leave room for something beyond paying to stay there.

00:09:31.030 --> 00:09:35.165
The goal is to enjoy the home you worked for, with enough

00:09:35.190 --> 00:09:36.890
breathing room to keep it.
